WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.400
- [Narrator] This is a run on a bank.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.710
This is also a run on a bank.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.110
And while these people lost their money,

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.910
these people didn't

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.617
thanks to a government
agency designed back then.

00:00:11.617 --> 00:00:14.610
- [President Roosevelt] We
do not want and will not have

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.280
another epidemic of bank failures.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.280
- [Narrator] The FDIC exists
to ensure your money is safe

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.430
and that bank closures
don't rattle the US economy.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:27.330
Here's how it works and what
happens when your bank fails.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.970
Let's start back with
these people in the middle

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.470
of the Great Depression.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.480
They were afraid their banks
would go out of business

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.220
and their money would vanish,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.310
so they'd run to withdraw it all first.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.470
But the issue, as President
Franklin Roosevelt

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.555
explained at the time, was-

00:00:42.555 --> 00:00:44.460
- [President Roosevelt] When
you deposit money in a bank,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.390
the bank does not put the money
into a safe deposit vault.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:52.050
It invests your money in many
different forms of credit.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.400
- [Narrator] He means
your bank doesn't have

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.090
enough cash on hand to pay out
all their accounts at once,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.160
not back then or today.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.580
So during a run, the bank
would have to suspend business

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.950
and most would then completely fail.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.450
Thousands of them did,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.270
which just made other people
panic about their money,

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.070
so they'd run to their bank.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.630
- And that's how a bank run on one bank

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.560
can become contagious to other banks.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.860
- [Narrator] So FDR signed
the Banking Act of 1933,

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.500
creating the Federal Deposit
Insurance Corporation,

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.170
the FDIC, to make people have faith

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.510
their money was safe in a US bank.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:31.837
It worked.

00:01:31.837 --> 00:01:33.633
- [President Roosevelt] Your
government does not intend

00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:37.920
that the history of the past
few years shall be repeated.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.160
- [Narrator] The FDIC is what
it sounds like, insurance.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.260
For every $100 you deposit,

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.060
your bank has to put a few cents

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.150
into the FDIC'S Deposit Insurance Fund.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.910
This is what's used to
reimburse you, the depositor,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.720
should your bank fail, but
only up to a certain amount.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.320
Currently, if you have
$250,000 or less in a bank,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.240
all that money is insured.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.540
Any amount in a bank account
over that is uninsured.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.870
You could in theory lose
it if the bank fails.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.270
This applies not only
to personal accounts,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.030
but also to businesses.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.790
When a bank is on the
brink of failure, the FDIC,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.820
along with a regulatory
agency, gets involved.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.620
Typically, they arrange to sell the bank

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.580
to another usually larger bank,
then close the failed bank,

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.320
trying to make the process seamless

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.910
for the bank's customers.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.510
- Taking the failed bank,
selling it to a healthy bank

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.690
will ensure that there's
continuity of services

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.120
to the community that the bank serves,

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.830
not just protecting insured deposits,

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.990
which the FDIC always does,

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.000
but usually the buyers would pay enough

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.260
to cover the uninsured, too.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.170
They wanted the uninsured 'cause
those were large deposits.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.060
And then also continue
servicing the loans,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.260
keep the credit lines open,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.230
you know, all the services
the bank provides.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.063
- [Narrator] Over the years,

00:02:53.063 --> 00:02:54.900
most failed banks have been small.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.210
Here they are skilled by size,

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.750
except for this one,
Washington Mutual in 2008,

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.720
the largest bank failure in US history.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:04.890
Most of the accounts,

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.620
primarily personal accounts, were insured,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.860
and the bank was sold for enough
money to cover the insured,

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.350
no need to even touch the Deposit Fund,

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.200
but also cover the uninsured accounts.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.430
But the bank failures of
2023 haven't been routine.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.550
With Silicon Valley Bank, the
vast majority of depositors

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.550
had accounts with more than
$250,000, so they're uninsured.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.110
It's a lot of businesses,

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.570
and because the bank
began to fail so quickly,

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.700
the FDIC hadn't lined up a buyer

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.170
before they had to close it.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:40.260
- Their deposit base was
a very close-knit group

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.660
of VCs and their portfolio companies,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.310
and word just spread like fire.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.630
You know, when a couple
of 'em got worried,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.070
they all started to run.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.630
- [Narrator] So the US announced-

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.270
- All customers who had
deposits in these banks

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.290
can rest assured,

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.810
I wanna rest assured they'll be protected.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.330
- [Narrator] Experts say
guaranteeing not just the insured

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.860
but also the uninsured

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.410
was aimed at stopping any
further economic damage

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.780
from these businesses not making payroll,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.100
but also might have stopped

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.380
other uninsured account
holders from running.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.060
- If the uninsured depositors in bank A

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.550
can't access their funds,

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.700
that's going to make the depositors

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.760
in bank B nervous.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.960
We have contagion, and
then we're back to 1933.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.910
- [Narrator] Reimbursing
all these depositors

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.610
will come from the
eventual sale of the bank

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.130
and also from the insurance fund.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.160
Many experts are worried the
amount banks have to contribute

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.580
may go up, so your bank fees could, too,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.390
a process that while messy
worked the way it was designed

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:43.927
nearly 100 years ago.

00:04:43.927 --> 00:04:45.978
- [President Roosevelt] I
can assure you, my friends,

00:04:45.978 --> 00:04:50.430
that it is safer to keep
your money in a reopened bank

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.220
than it is to keep it under the mattress.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:58.220
- I think on the one hand, FDR
would be wincing and saying,

00:04:59.078 --> 00:05:03.990
"Oh man, I thought we wouldn't
have screw ups like this.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:08.677
On the other hand, I think
he would smile and say,

00:05:08.677 --> 00:05:11.167
"Ah, it didn't spread.

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:13.237
"We haven't had contagion.

00:05:13.237 --> 00:05:16.027
"We haven't had widespread runs.

00:05:16.027 --> 00:05:20.037
"That was my goal in creating the FDIC."

